<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ganapati (Kakatiya dynasty)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 1199–<a href="/years/1262">1262</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Ganapati Deva, Ganapatideva</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ganapatideva was the longest-reigning ruler of the Kakatiya dynasty, governing from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warangal">Warangal</a> from 1199 to 1262. He united most Telugu-speaking lands, extending Kakatiya rule to the coastal Andhra country, and encouraged foreign trade through the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/motupalli">Motupalli</a>. His reign saw large irrigation tanks and temples built, and he was succeeded by his daughter Rudramadevi.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ganapati-kakatiya.jpg" alt="Ganapati (Kakatiya dynasty)" width="320" height="514" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Skorthiw at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Warangal_fort.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
